<Layout
  title="Projects | Joe Fazio"
  description="Work Joe Fazio has built: a time-banking system, hands-on security labs, a risk assessment, and this site."
>
  <h1>Projects</h1>

  <p>
    Five projects. Four grew out of my final year at James Madison University,
    and the fifth is this site. The employers, dates and courses behind them are
    on my <a href="/resume.pdf">resume</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>ACC Timebank</h2>
  <p class="meta">Spring 2026 · lead developer · Google Apps Script, Google Sheets, Python</p>

  <p>
    A time-banking system for the Alachua Community Collective, a Florida
    mutual-aid group whose members trade hours of work instead of money. I was
    lead developer on the fast, low-cost track and did most of the requirements
    gathering across the engagement. Our team, Purple Peak Consulting, received
    the KPMG Choice Award for Best Overall System. It appears on my resume as the
    Quick Hit Timebank System.
  </p>

  <p class="more">
    <a href="/projects/acc-timebank">Read the case study</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Ethical Hacking Lab Course</h2>
  <p class="meta">Spring 2026 · JMU · VirtualBox, Kali Linux, Metasploit, Nessus</p>

  <p>
    A semester of hands-on security labs inside an isolated virtual network I
    built for the purpose: a Kali Linux machine and a Windows target on a private
    adapter with no route to the outside, so nothing could touch anything real.
    The labs covered defense in depth, keylogger detection, social engineering,
    PGP, hash-based integrity checks, and a Nessus vulnerability scan whose
    deliverable was a triaged, prioritized set of fixes rather than raw findings.
  </p>

  <h2>BRHS Cybersecurity Risk Assessment</h2>
  <p class="meta">Spring 2026 · co-author · NIST CSF 2.0</p>

  <p>
    A consulting report for Blue Ridge Health Services, a fictional healthcare
    provider written for the assignment: identify the weaknesses, rank them, and
    recommend hardening a real budget could absorb. We chose NIST's Cybersecurity
    Framework over CIS Controls v8: its outcome-based structure could be fitted to
    a hospital's workflow, where a fixed checklist would have fought it. This was
    coursework, and the method is the part that carries over.
  </p>

  <h2>Capsim Business Simulation</h2>
  <p class="meta">Spring 2026 · strategic management · R&amp;D</p>

  <p>
    A College of Business simulation in which a cross-functional team runs a
    company over multiple rounds, setting pricing, R&amp;D, marketing and
    production each round and carrying the results into the next. My teammates
    were marketing, management and accounting majors, and I ran R&amp;D, revising
    our product designs each round to keep them inside the shifting buying
    criteria for our segments. Working alongside three other disciplines was the
    point: it taught me how a technical decision reads to the people who have to
    sell, staff and account for it.
  </p>

  <h2>This site</h2>
  <p class="meta">2026 · ongoing · Astro, Cloudflare Pages, GitHub Actions</p>

  <p>
    A static site on Astro, hosted on Cloudflare Pages, with DNS and TLS I manage
    myself. Every push runs a build in GitHub Actions, and a branch rule blocks
    anything from reaching the main branch unless that build passes. The
    repository is public and carries a decision log with a written reason for
    every choice in it, including the ones I reversed.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/joefazio1/joefazio-portfolio">Source on GitHub</a>
  </p>
</Layout>

